<article class="env-lemma" id="0B7C">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0B7C">Lemma <span data-tag="0B7C">10.19.2</span>.</a> Let $\varphi : R \to S$ be a ring map such that the induced map $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(S) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$ is surjective. Then an element $x \in R$ is a unit if and only if $\varphi (x) \in S$ is a unit. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $x$ is a unit, then so is $\varphi (x)$. Conversely, if $\varphi (x)$ is a unit, then $\varphi (x) \not\in \mathfrak q$ for all $\mathfrak q \in \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(S)$. Hence $x \not\in \varphi ^{-1}(\mathfrak q) = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\varphi )(\mathfrak q)$ for all $\mathfrak q \in \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(S)$. Since $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\varphi )$ is surjective we conclude that $x$ is a unit by part (17) of Lemma <a href="/tag/00E0" data-tag="00E0">10.17.2</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>